# Build output. `crewlet` is what `go build ./cmd/crewlet` drops in the
# working directory; dist/ is goreleaser's.
/crewlet
/dist/

# Python bytecode. This tree has not held a `.py` file since the Go rewrite,
# which is exactly why the rule was missing and exactly why it is needed: the
# pre-Go checkout left `__pycache__` directories behind as UNTRACKED debris,
# and they survived every branch switch afterwards because git does not remove
# what it never tracked. A `git add -A` while resolving a merge then committed
# 623 `.pyc` files under `src/` and `tests/` -- a `src/` tree whose every
# source file had been deleted years earlier, restored as bytecode alone. They
# reached `main`, and this is a public repository, so deleting them does not
# unpublish them; git history keeps what a later commit drops.
#
# So the pattern stays even with no Python in the tree. Whatever a contributor
# ran to produce those caches -- a stale virtualenv, a tool reaching into an
# old checkout -- can produce them again, and the failure is silent in both
# directions: nothing builds Python here, so no build goes red, and the files
# are unreadable binary, so no diff review catches them.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]

# The store is a local file this node owns exclusively, and the embedded
# stream persists beside it. Both are per-checkout state, never committed.
*.db
*.db-shm
*.db-wal
# The lock sidecar `store.Open` writes beside the database file — see
# internal/store/lock.go. It is `<database>.lock`, so it lands wherever the
# database does; one reached this public repository as `internal/store/.lock`,
# carrying a pid, a hostname and a timestamp.
#
# `*.db.lock` and not `*.lock`: the broad pattern would silently swallow
# yarn.lock, Cargo.lock, uv.lock and every other dependency manifest, and a
# manifest nobody can see is a surface nobody watches -- it gets no
# Dependabot entry and nothing reports the omission. This is exactly as
# broad as the `*.db` above it, which is the thing it shadows.
*.db.lock
/crewlet-data/
/nimbus-data/
/acme-data/

.env
# Minted-credential env files the provisioning CLIs write from the repo
# root (.env.gitlab, .env.mattermost, any operator variant) — never
# committable.
.env.*
# ...except the committed template.
!.env.example

# The Slack provisioning ledger `crewlet slack provision` writes beside a
# company document. It holds the app client secrets and signing secrets
# Slack serves once, at creation, and the operator's app-configuration
# token pair — so it is a secrets file exactly like .env, and committing
# it publishes credentials nothing can rotate for you.
slack-apps.json
**/slack-apps.json

# Key material never belongs in git. A `stream.tls` block for an external
# NATS server names a private CA bundle and a client keypair (see
# docs/guides/deployment.md), and those are files an operator drops beside the
# config in a checkout — so the patterns are blanket rather than per-recipe.
*.key
*.pem

# Locally-pointed copies of the example company config that the integration
# guides' local-testing recipes create (and that operators then personalize
# with real contact IDs) — see docs/integrations/gitlab.md.
*.local.company.yaml

# Git worktrees an AI coding assistant creates to isolate parallel work
# (Claude Code's `isolation: worktree`). Each is a full nested checkout with
# its own .git pointer — transient scaffolding for one task, and committing
# one would put a checkout inside the repository it was taken from. The rest
# of .claude/ is deliberately NOT ignored: settings.json and any skills there
# are project configuration and belong in the tree.
.claude/worktrees/

.idea/
.vscode/
.DS_Store

# The dashboard's dependency tree. Its BUILD OUTPUT is deliberately NOT ignored
# — static/dashboard is committed, because `go build ./...` and
# `go install …@latest` must work on a clean checkout with no node on the
# machine and an embed directive cannot run a bundler. CI rebuilds it and diffs
# the tree, so a stale commit is a red build rather than a silent one.
dashboard/node_modules/
